    historyContent: {
      origins: 'Documented in London publications as early as 1793 (e.g., The Sporting Magazine). It was designed as a high-stakes gambling game for two people who wanted the rigor of Whist without needing partners.',
      originCountries: [
        'England',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '1790s: First mentioned in British sporting journals.',
        '19th Century: Gradually replaced by German Whist, which introduced hidden information.',
        'Present: Studied by game historians as a precursor to modern 2-player trick-taking games.',
      ],
      evolution: "The game's 'Humbug' name likely refers to the deceptive nature of lead strategies in a game where both players know the full deck state.",
      cultural: 'Often played in clubs and informal settings for high stakes due to its clinical, non-random nature.',
    },
    setupContent: {
      players: 'Exactly two players. Humbug was designed as a high-stakes two-hand whist variant for two people who wanted the rigor of Whist without partners; it is documented in London publications from the 1790s (e.g. The Sporting Magazine).',
